course: dictionary
lesson: 8356
title: Rota: taking turns
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A rota (say ROH-tah) is a list that shows who has to do a job and when, so that everyone takes a turn. Schools use rotas for tidying up, watering plants and being class monitors.
explain: «A family rota, where each person chooses the film in turn, makes movie night fair.» Here the rota is not about chores. It is a fair way of sharing the choice, so nobody always gets their way. Look at the words around rota: «in turn» and «fair» tell you it is about taking turns.
explain: Shops, hospitals, buses and factories need people at all hours, so their workers follow a rota. The rota can change every week, so the shifts are shared out. When you meet rota in a text, ask: is this a list of who does what, and when?
fact: A rota is a list showing who does a job and when.
fact: The idea of a rota is that people take turns, so the work or the fun is shared fairly.
fact: In the UK, Australia and New Zealand, people say «rota». In the USA, people often say «roster» or «schedule» for the same idea.
fact: Everyday rotas include cleaning rotas, duty rotas and watering rotas for school plants.
fact: Phrases you may hear: «a cleaning rota», «on the rota», «draw up a rota».
exercise: «A family rota, where each person chooses the film in turn, makes movie night fair.» What is a rota? | A film about the sea | *A list showing who does a job and when, so people take turns | A loud argument about who sits on the sofa | A kind of cinema seat
exercise: Why do hospitals and buses use a rota? | Because they like to change their buildings every week | Because rotas are cheaper than paper | Because workers want to stop working | *Because people are needed at all hours, so the work is shared out
exercise: In the USA, people say «roster» or «schedule» instead of rota. Which word could an American use for a rota? | toque | *roster | chur | bud
test: Ella's name is on the washing-up rota this week. What does «on the rota» mean? | She has lost the list of jobs | She has finished washing up for good | She is allowed to skip every job | *Her name is on the list for a job this week
test: What does it mean to «draw up a rota»? | To draw a picture of a clock | To write a letter to a friend | To argue about who should win | *To make a list showing who does a job and when
exam: «Either you feed the hamster or he starves!» Why is this unfair when there is a rota on the fridge? | The word either is too long | *It pretends there are only two choices, when a rota gives many fair options | Hamsters should never be fed | Fridges are not allowed in kitchens
